<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of San Gimignano</h1><p class="st-meta">1199 CE – 1353 CE (approx.) · capital San Gimignano</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Tuscan hill-town republic on the pilgrim road to Rome, whose surviving medieval towers make it the best-preserved image of a communal city.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>San Gimignano grew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a>, the pilgrim route from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and won self-government from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-volterra">Volterra</a>; by 1199 it was an independent commune under consuls.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The town prospered from pilgrims, trade and especially saffron, grown in the surrounding hills and sold as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. Rival families, led by the Ardinghelli and the Salvucci, built tower houses as symbols of wealth and power; around 72 are said to have stood, and 14 survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> came as an envoy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a> in 1300. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegiata-san-gimignano">Collegiata</a> church was frescoed by Sienese painters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 and the decline of the pilgrim trade left the town poor and exhausted by feuds. In 1353 it submitted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence</a>, and its towers survived because it stopped growing.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commune-of-san-gimignano.jpg" alt="Commune of San Gimignano" width="320" height="225"><figcaption>Image: Chensiyuan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_Aerial_pano_san_gimignano_(2)a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1199 CE – 1353 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Gimignano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuscan Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>San Gimignano</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1276&amp;focus=commune-of-san-gimignano" aria-label="Commune of San Gimignano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4946 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of San Gimignano lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5266 892L5270 883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5266 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5266 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1276&amp;focus=commune-of-san-gimignano">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
